{"type":"exercise","type_name":"Exercise","number":"","title":"A1","title_html":"A1","html":"<p>A colleague reports a ridge-penalized logistic fit with <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\lambda=100\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D706 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> on standardized covariates.\nWhat normal prior does that correspond to, and what odds ratio for a one\nstandard deviation change does the prior regard as, say, two standard\ndeviations from zero?</p>","file":"ch39-glms-in-practice-bayes/05-priors-as-regularizers.html"}